{
  "id": 6,
  "slug": "storm-front",
  "title": "Storm Front",
  "concept": "Surviving the loss of an Availability Zone: a fleet across two zones, a Multi-AZ RDS database that fails over to its standby, and a database security group that names the application tier rather than one zone's addresses.",
  "guidance": "hints",
  "region": "eu-central-1",
  "azs": 2,
  "lockedIds": ["igw", "alb", "rt-public", "rt-private", "nacl-default", "sg-alb"],
  "palette": [],
  "removableIds": [],
  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "brief",
      "text": "Storm Front. Eight Cloudlings are checking their book fair bookings, through the load balancer to the web fleet, which reads from the bookings database. Both live only in Availability Zone a, the left-hand column, and part-way through the day AZ a will fail. Read the clouds' cards, then fly the kites and watch the storm.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "what-happened",
      "text": "When AZ a failed, everything in it went down: the fleet's two instances, and the only copy of the database. The load balancer had nothing healthy to send kites to, and the fleet had nowhere else to launch. Three changes let the application ride out a storm like this, and the next steps take them one at a time.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "spread",
      "text": "First, the web fleet. Click it and tick Private subnet B as well, so it runs instances in both Availability Zones. Then, if AZ a fails, the load balancer still has instances in AZ b, and the group can launch replacements there.",
      "highlight": "resource:asg",
      "pulse": ["group-subnets:asg"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "group-spans-zones",
          "groupId": "asg",
          "zones": 2
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "multi-az",
      "text": "Next, the database. Click it and switch on Multi-AZ. RDS then keeps a standby copy in AZ b, updated with every write, ready to take over if AZ a fails.",
      "highlight": "resource:db",
      "pulse": ["database-multi-az:db"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "database-multi-az",
          "databaseId": "db"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "chain",
      "text": "One more trap, and it is easy to miss. The database's security group, rds-db-sg, allows MySQL 3306 only from 10.0.11.0/24, which is Private subnet A's address range. Instances in AZ b would be turned away. Chain it instead: allow MySQL 3306 from the app tier's security group, ec2-app-sg, and remove the address-range rule.",
      "highlight": "resource:db",
      "pulse": ["security-group:sg-db"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "security-group-allows-group",
          "securityGroupId": "sg-db",
          "ports": "mysql",
          "fromSecurityGroupId": "sg-app"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "second-run",
      "text": "Fly the kites again. Watch the lightning over AZ a, the database fail over to its standby in AZ b, and the fleet carry on from AZ b. CloudWatch's log on the right says what happens, minute by minute.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wrap",
      "text": "AZ a went down and the application stayed up: the fleet carried on in AZ b, the database failed over, and its security group still knew the app tier. No third star yet? It is for being [statically stable](card:concept-static-stability): on the web fleet's card, raise the minimum to 4, two instances per zone, so AZ b alone can carry the crowd. Then fly again.",
      "pulse": ["group-size:asg"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "level-complete"
      }
    }
  ],
